## 1. Introduction
It has been ${>}30$ years since the concept of an infinite polymer framework was first proposed. On this basis, the term metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) in the 1990s was coined, and the past decades have witnessed the vigorous development of research in this field (Kitagawa, 2014). Metal ions as well as organic ligands self-assemble into MOFs with porous structures by forming coordination bonds. Due to the fact that the structure, surface area, as well as function of MOFs might be modified by adjusting the reaction conditions, MOFs have great potential in different fields, such as sensing, analysis, catalysis, active ingredient delivery, etc. (La´zaro & Forgan, 2019; Zhao et al., 2022a). MOFs have rich geometric structures and connectivity by virtue of metal ions/ligands, and then form periodic structures with infinite networks through coordination or covalent bonds (Cook et al., 2013). The inorganic and organic moieties govern the activities and structures of MOFs because they integrate their respective roles to generate coor­ dination crystals. However, the biological application of porous MOFs must address both the function and biocompatibility of the material. Regrettably, several MOFs published to date are reliant on hazardous metals (e.g., Co, Ni, Cr, Gd, etc.), as well as the bioactivity/toxicity in­ formation of organic bridges is quite limited, which impedes their deployment, particularly in medical applications.
The majority of MOFs are produced via solvothermal techniques, which typically involve heating a combination of metal salts and organic ligands together within a solvent. In addition to the more common solvothermal approach, additional effective methods of synthesis include microwave, mechanochemistry, sonochemistry, as well as electrochemistry (Al Amery et al., 2020). However, none of the MOF preparation procedures are reusable, and the synthesized components' elevated toxicity (metal ions and organic ligands) or the chosen chem­ ical reagents severely restrict the MOFs practical industrial application. In addition, with the increasingly urgent need for materials that can be applied to biology in a timely manner, researchers are becoming inter­ ested in green and renewable bio-metal framework materials. Currently, the biomolecules used as building blocks in these biometallic frame­ works are usually made of nontoxic, degradable raw materials, such as amino acids, peptides, and polysaccharides. Compared with the nonrenewable ligands used in traditional MOFs synthesis, the biological li­ gands used at this stage have multiple coordination sites themselves, which can properly bind to metal ions. In addition, these ligand mole­ cules also possess the characteristics of unique cavity structure, good biocompatibility, easy availability, and easy modification. The addition of biological ligands can not only reduce the toxicity of MOFs but also customize functional composites. Therefore, bio-based MOFs have promising uses in gas storage, catalysis, sensing, separation, as well as biomedicine, etc., in addition to new biomedical opportunities (Bilal et al., 2019; Nadar et al., 2019).
Cyclodextrins (CDs) are considered cyclic oligosaccharides that are produced by starch enzymatic breakdown and play an extremely important role in supramolecular chemistry (Tian et al., 2022). Natu­ rally occurring CDs are homogeneous crystals formed by $^{\alpha-1,4}$ glycosidic bonds and are mainly divided into $\upalpha\cdot$ -, $\upbeta\cdot$ , and $\gamma$ -CD. Since their discovery, the pharmaceutical, cosmetic, and food sectors have been the primary users of CDs because of their ability to manage the distribution of active substances and their solubilization. As reported, the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) as well as the European Medicines Agency (EMA) have ratified natural and some CD derivatives as safe additives. Based on the special groups in the CD structure, they can coordinate with alkali metal particles to form CD-MOFs. Since the first synthesis and structural characterization of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOFs by Stoddart et al. in 2010, a large number of studies have confirmed that $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOFs have the character­ istics of easy synthesis, easy modification, and low toxicity (Roy & Stoddart, 2021). MOFs constructed with $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ have an open porous framework that enables them to support high loads. Due to the more symmetrical structure, the $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ frequency in the MOFs synthesis ex­ ceeds that of. As a result of advances in research and technology, ma­ terials consisting of $\alpha\cdot$ and $\upbeta$ -CD have also been produced and described. Their uses range from molecule adsorption and separation to sensing and molecular recognition, memristor fabrication, the creation of nanoparticles, light emission, and medication administration (Rajkumar et al., 2019; Shen et al., 2021). Porous materials created from affordable green resources considerably expand MOFs' food industry growth po­ tential. CD-MOFs made from food-grade CDs and metal ions are harm­ less, which might solve MOFs' safety concerns.
In this paper, synthesizing CD-MOFs and exploring their potential uses in the delivery, recognition, separation, and capture of active molecules are reviewed. First, the characteristics and preparation methods of MOFs are briefly described and summarized. From the perspective of different CDs, the participation of CDs in the preparation of MOFs was compared. In terms of the safety issue, current reports about CD-MOFs are summarized. Finally, existing and upcoming ob­ stacles to using CD-MOFs in different sectors, especially chemical en­ gineering and biomaterial science, are clarified.
## 2. The synthesis of MOFs
In traditional MOFs synthesis methods, most cationic elements in the periodic table are ruled out as potential biological building blocks. $({\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ , $\bar{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , etc.) in the preparation process (Nong et al., 2020). Different kinds of MOFs materials have been synthesized using different metal ions, organic linkers, and solvents that affect structural and hydrothermal stability. Due to the metals' varied valence states and the organic links' density, it has been shown that each metal has distinct inorganic structures or clusters; resulting in various topol­ ogies and networks. Furthermore, some of these materials are known to undergo structural transformations upon the removal of solvent mole­ cules during activation. Therefore, after solvent molecules removal, the volume of the unit cell may shrink due to the stronger p-p stacking within the organic linker's aromatic rings. Such as, Some MIL-53s including the MIL-53 (Al, Cr, Fe) possess dynamical characteristics: that is, the pore size changes from small to large upon dehydration and hydration, and vice versa. The behavior found in MOFs is known as structural respiration, which broadens their practical scope. This dy­ namic is affected by (guest interaction), (host-guest interaction), as well as applied pressure. Due to its linkers and metal clusters, MOFs are frequently utilized. Two kinds of MOF synthesis exist: liquid-phase synthesis methods (slow evaporation, hydrothermal/solvothermal treatment, electrochemical/sonochemical, microwave, spray drying, flow chemistry, and ionothermal methods) as well as solid-phase syn­ thesis (mechanical grinding) (Safaei et al., 2019) (Fig. 1).
When preparing MOFs by liquid-phase synthesis, it is necessary to mix the selective solvent, metal salt, also suitable organic linker inside a suitable container. The selection of suitable solvents is decisive for the preparation of MOFs, such as solubility, reactivity, stability, donor quantity, dielectric constant, polarity, besides boiling temperature. Solid-phase synthesis refers to compounds that are produced mechani­ cally under free or low solvent conditions. Table 1 summarizes the characteristics of different types of synthesis methods.
## 3. Synthesis methods and application of CD-MOFs
MOFs prepared with different kinds of CDs are reported to have different sizes. Interestingly, by using various metal ions, CD-MOFs' physical characteristics may be adjusted ( $\mathbf{\widetilde{\mathbf{\Gamma}}}_{\overline{{\mathbf{\Gamma}}}}\mathbf{\Psi}_{\overline{{\mathbf{\Gamma}}}}$ -KCD-MOF: $24.13\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -NaCDMOF: 32.86 $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ , and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -FeCD-MOF: $30.19\mathrm{~\normalfont~\AA~}$ . Therefore, exploring different preparation methods can lay the foundation for the wider CDMOFs application (Fig. 1). Owing to the several preparation methods' summary (Table 2), it is found that the solvent diffusion method is widely used in the preparation of MOF materials with different types of CDs due to its simple operation, safety, and mild reaction conditions.
Due to the porosity of CD-MOFs, more and more small molecules can be “loaded” by the pore-like structure so that they have several appli­ cations such as medication administration, gas capture, food packaging, compound separation and so on (Fig. 2). In this section, the application fields of CD-MOFs are divided into four main aspects: delivery, detec­ tion, isolation and capture for a detailed summary description (Table 3).
## 3.1. Delivery
CD-MOFs offer a wide range of applications in the delivery of active substances. A large number of basic studies have confirmed that different drug molecules can be effectively delivered via CD-MOF, thereby improving the application prospects of materials and drugs. Li et al. used solvent diffusion to synthesize the $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF and loaded triptolide (TPL) as the active ingredient $(308.19\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ . In vitro release experiment, it was found that the release of triptolide reached $97\ \%$ in $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . To validate the unique mode of action of the medication loaded by the modified substance, the researchers used cell uptake (Huh-7 cells), cell survival (Huh-7 cells), and pharmacokinetics $(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}}~=~1.2~\mathrm{h})$ . Importantly, the findings of anti-cancer tests revealed that its anti-tumor activity in vitro as well as in vivo was somewhat greater, and it was statistically significant (Li et al., 2022b). (Fig. 3) To increase ibuprofen oral bioavailability and absorption rate, Hartlieb et al. presented an ibuprofen-co-crystal with a biocompatible (CD-MOF) which did not substantially affect cell viability. Bioavailability studies were performed in mice comparing the ibuprofen/CD-MOF medication co-crystals to control samples of ibuprofen potassium salt in the presence and absence of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD. Obviously, the ibuprofen/CD-MOF-1 co-crystal exhibited similar quick assimilation of ibuprofen as the ibuprofen potassium salt control sample. In $20~\mathrm{min}$ , the maximal plasma concentrations were recorded, as well as the co-crystal had one in the plasma sample. The extra advantage of keeping a $100\%$ higher half-life was that it was less hygroscopic than pure salt (Hartlieb et al., 2017).
Sulfasalazine is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory and antirheumatic drug widely used in the therapy of rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, enteropathy, Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis. The oral bioavailability (about $10{-}30\%$ ) of sulfasalazine is rather low because of its instability, poor water solubility as well as low membrane perme­ ability. Agafonov et al. proposed the delivery of sulfasalazine by impregnation and co-crystallization method by loading it into $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CDMOF. By simulating in vitro dissolution studies in gastric $(\mathrm{pH}=1.6\$ ) and intestinal fluids $\mathrm{(pH=6.8)}$ ), the experimental results revealed that the drug was completely released within $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ under acidic conditions; while $80\%$ was released within $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ under neutral conditions. Therefore, researchers have made great efforts to weaken the burst release of the drug from the material (PBS, $\mathrm{pH}=6.8\dot{}$ ), and the results reported that the addition of ethylcellulose led to a delayed release of the drug (Agafonov et al., 2022).
In the study of iodine delivery studies, Chen et al. successfully pre­ pared KI-CD-MOF scaffolds. Further, iodine loading $(30~\mathrm{wt\%})$ was found to be efficiently achieved using gas-solid reaction, and mechanism studies revealed that the encapsulation of iodine and CD molecules synergistically enhanced the solubility and stability of iodine (Chen et al., 2022a). Another study also displayed that the release time of hydrogel containing iodine in artificial saliva could be extended up to 5 d. Based on rat periodontitis model, microcomputed tomography of alveolar bone morphology revealed that such hydrogel was similar to the periodontitis antibiotic minocycline ointment in reducing peri­ odontal pocket depth and alveolar bone resorption (Lu et al., 2021).
In another study, curcumin was selected as a model drug, and $\upbeta$ -CDMOF material was applied as delivery vehicle for further studies. $\upbeta$ -CDMOF material showed a maximum drug loading of $18.33\%$ for curcumin and in vitro release studies showed a cumulative release of up to $81\%$ within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . In addition, simulated in vitro gastrointestinal release studies revealed a cumulative drug release of $50\%$ within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which demonstrated that the combination of MOF and polymer had great po­ tential for drug delivery (Shao et al., 2022). In addition, nanoscale $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CDMOF-1 was prepared to deliver curcumin, and dissolution experiments confirmed that curcumin was uniformly dispersed from top to bottom during dissolution. The release performance of the material was controlled by the curcumin loading. Additionally, the antioxidant sta­ bility of curcumin loaded in MOF materials was significantly improved after $120\mathrm{min}$ of UV irradiation. Regarding the application of $\gamma$ -CD-MOF1 material in future food products, Chen et al. used an improved sol­ vothermal method and PEG-20000 to effectively deliver and protect curcumin. Curcumin loaded in MOFs material has good encapsulation efficiency $(34.03\pm2.27\ \%)$ and loading capacity $(7.52\pm1.90\ \%)$ . Therefore, this material can be used as a food packaging active material in future food applications (Chen et al., 2022c). Encapsulation of cate­ chin into nanoscale CD-MOFs could protect catechin from light, oxygen and temperature, thus improving its storage stability. According to the data of pharmacological experiments, catechin encapsulated in CDMOFs had better bioavailability $(>40\%)$ (Jiang et al., 2022).
$\gamma$ -CD-MOF materials could be synthesized rapidly $(15~\mathrm{{min})}$ by ul­ trasound assistance for caffeic acid delivery. $19\%$ loading of caffeic acid was achieved by $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF, which was twice as high as $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD. Release experiments in ethanol indicated that the in vitro release behavior of caffeic acid was in accordance with the Higuchi model (Shen et al., 2022). Cinnamaldehyde has good antimicrobial activity, however, it is limited by its unstable and perishable properties. A biocompatible carbon-dot hybridized $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF was prepared by a seed-mediated approach, and the loading of cinnamaldehyde in the MOF material was $28.42~\%$ , and the sustained release time at $8~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was $>15\mathrm{~d~}$ . The release kinetic results reported that the drug release behavior was consistent with the Korsmeyer-Peppas release kinetic model, indicating that it was mainly released by diffusion-controlled slow release. Treat­ ment with drug-loaded $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF was able to extend the edibility of fresh-cut cantaloupes up to $^{5\mathrm{d}}$ (Che et al., 2022).
The study was carried out to solve the yield problem of menthol inclusion complexes, and MOF material based on $\upbeta$ -CD was prepared. The menthol/ $\mathrm{^{\prime}\beta}$ -CD-MOF-IC was prepared by grinding method according to optimized conditions (temperature $90\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ ; ratio 1:1; reaction time 60 min), and the menthol content and encapsulation rate were 27.1 and $30.6~\%$ , respectively. The results of thermogravimetric experiments revealed that the encapsulated compounds loaded in the $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF material had stronger heat resistance and greater moisture sensitivity compared with the encapsulated compounds. In the simulated oral release experiments, the menthol release rates of different samples were in the order of: pure menthol $<$ inclusion complex $\mathrm{<MOF}$ material loaded menthol (Hu et al., 2022).
Kritskiy and his co-workers successfully synthesized $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD-MOF}}$ by gas-phase diffusion, in which they could encapsulate leflunomide. The researcher found that the solubility of the drug was reduced under acidic conditions, while it was significantly enhanced under neutral conditions $\mathrm{(>0.008~mol/kg)}$ . In addition, in vitro release experiments indicated that the full release of the drug could be achieved within $50~\mathrm{min}$ , thus $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF could be a new method for the administration of leflunomide (Kritskiy et al., 2019). Using supercritical carbon dioxide technology to activate $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF, it was discovered that this method can be obtained with higher surface area and greater pore volume. Besides, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}.$ - assisted impregnation method was used to introduce honokiol (insoluble drug) into CD-MOF pores, with the former having a higher drug loading $(40.78\ \%)$ compared with conventional liquid-phase doping with ethanol. Remarkably, intestinal cell uptake and transport experiments showed that CD-MOF enhanced uptake by intestinal epithelial cells and trans-intestinal drug transport compared to cyclodextrin inclusion complexes. In addition, in vivo pharmacokinetic studies farther verified that CD-MOF markedly enhanced the oral absorption and bioavailability of honokiol $(\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}}=0.56~\mathrm{h}$ ; $\mathrm{C_{max}}=0.85\:\mathrm{mg/L)}$ (He et al., 2020).
Targeted delivery of therapeutic agents or visualizers to injured vessels via nanocarriers may be dependent on the shape of the particles, but cubic nanocarriers have not been reported for vascular targeting. Here, researchers demonstrated that cubic CD scaffolds have better he­ mostatic effect and injury vascular targeting compared to spherical shape. Cubic and biocompatible $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOFs were synthesized by crosslinking and surface modification of GRGDS peptides (GS5-MOFs). The specific interaction of cubic GS5-MOF nanoparticles with activated platelets was investigated by in vitro platelet aggregation assay and atomic force microscopy (AFM). Enhanced adhesion and aggregation of MOF nanoparticles with activated platelets were found by observational analysis under in vitro static conditions and physiologically relevant flow environment. Interestingly, MOF nanoparticles had effective he­ mostasis with $90\%$ reduction in bleeding time and bleeding volume, and in vivo vascular targeting, which was significantly better than spherical $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ nanosponges with the same chemical composition (He et al., 2019b).
## 3.2. Detection
Effective identification of harmful chemical components provides a guarantee for the safety of food and drugs in the future. Tu et al. ob­ tained Mxene/carbon nanohorn/ $\upbeta$ -cyclodextrin-MOF (Mxene/CNHs/ $\upbeta$ -CDMOFs) such as carbendazim (CBZ) electrochemical sensing plat­ form for pesticide detection. According to the characterization results, the material combined the characteristics of CD host-guest recognition with porous structure, high porosity and pore volume, so CBZ had a high adsorption capacity. In terms of electrode performance studies, it was surprising to find that the linear range of the electrode was extended from $3.0\mathrm{nM}$ to $10.0\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ , with a $1.0\mathrm{nM}$ $(S/\Nu=3)$ ) lower limit of detection (LOD). In the tomato sample store experiment, it was found that the sample recovery rate was $97.77\ \%-102.01\ \%$ , and the relative standard deviation was $2.7\ \%-4.1\ \%$ , which confirmed the good applicability of the sensor (Tu et al., 2020). Similarly, Nikhar et al. prepared a $\upbeta$ -CDMOF, that was shown to be effective in detecting volatile organic mol­ ecules (benzene and nitrobenzene) (Nikhar et al., 2021).
Baicalin is a natural molecule with significant antiviral activity, its dose must be properly regulated; otherwise, excessive levels may pro­ duce severe physiological effects. Traditional detection methods mainly rely on traditional HPLC, LC-MS and other methods, However, those techniques have drawbacks such as increased expenses, difficult administration, and a time-consuming processing procedure. Zhao et al. designed a CD-MOF detector and found that under optimal conditions $(\mathrm{pH}=2.5$ ; 25 electrodeposition cycles; $0\mathrm{v}$ accumulation potential), the sensor had a high sensitivity for baicalin detection, and the linear range was $1\times10^{-9}{-}5\times10^{-7}\mathrm{M},$ , LOD of $4.6\times10^{-10}\mathrm{M}.$ , sensitivity of $220\mathrm{A}/\$ M, good stability as well as baicalin detection accuracy within real samples (human serum, bear bile and eye drops) (Zhao et al., 2022b) (Fig. 4).
With the help of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and alkali metal cations, Li and his colleagues successfully prepared $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF (Li et al., 2018). Surprisingly, the $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CDMOF was found to possess high selectivity and adsorption ability for amine compounds. To further explore the selectivity of the $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF, they chose sulfathiazole, sulfamethoxypyrimidine, sulfamethoxypyr­ imidine, sulfadimethoxypyrimidine and sulfoquinoline as the target analytes. The analytical results discovered that the $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF material had good enrichment ability for the five amines with a rapid adsorption equilibrium time $(30\ \mathrm{min})$ . Based on the optimized parameters, the spiked recoveries of meat samples (chicken, pork, liver and fish) ranged from $76\%$ to $102\%$ with the relative standard deviations of $2.4\ \%-6.5$ $\%$ , indicating that this method was appropriate to effectively extract amine compounds from complex matrices. The analysis of the adsorp­ tion mechanism in combination with HPLC revealed that the experi­ mental process was dominated by chemisorption. Furthermore, these researches' results were consistent with the chemisorption mechanism important in static adsorption experiments (Li et al., 2018).
Chirality is a common characteristic in nature, and effective discrimination of chiral molecules remains to be tough. Sun et al. experimented $\upbeta$ -CD as an organic ligand, ligated with $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ to form MOFs material, and then they used diphenyl carbonate to link MOFs to obtain chiral cross-linked MOFs. It was shown that this MOF material could sustain a stabilized structure in aqueous condition, and thus can expand its application scope. In addition, the $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF was co-modified with graphene oxide on a glassy carbon electrode surface to construct an electrochemical sensor for the enantiomeric recognition of phenyl­ ethanolic acid. The enantiomers of phenylethanoic acid exhibited different electrochemical behaviors on the sensor. The peak current ratio $\mathrm{(I_{L}/I_{D})}$ reached 1.8 and the peak potential difference ΔE $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{D}^{-}}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{L}})$
