Excellent adsorption performance and storage was $96~\mathrm{mV}$ . The method was well linear during $0.5{-}30~\mathrm{mM}$ , with the detection limit of $0.15\mathrm{mM}$ and $0.09\mathrm{mM}$ for the D and L conformations of phenylethanoic acid, respectively. This strategy was favourably uti­ lized to the determination of the enantiomers of phenylethanoic acid at unknown concentrations in urine. A related mechanistic analysis spec­ ulated that this separation might be due to the formation of di­ astereoisomers of phenylethanolic acid with $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF, making the identification of enantiomers on the electrode surface possible (Sun et al., 2022b).
To enhance detection ability of low concentrations of glucose, re­ searchers have successfully prepared MOF-235 heterodimer with $\upbeta$ -CD. The hybrid was found to possess high catalytic performance for the hydrogen peroxide-luminol system, and the chemiluminescence response was over 30-fold enhanced compared to the hydrogen peroxide-luminol system for the sensitive detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose. Analysis of the mechanism for the significant performance enhancement revealed the prominent catalytic activity of the heterocyte, and this was associated with the great specific surface area of MOF-235 and the mutual effect of $\upbeta$ -CD with MOF-235. Combining this detection method with chemiluminescence detection resulted in detection limit of ${\bf5}\ \mathrm{nM}$ and $10\mathrm{\nM}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and glucose, respectively. The MOF-235/ $\mathrm{^{\circ}}$ -CD complex proved to be a sensitive and effective catalyst for chemiluminescence-based analysis when successfully applied to the chemiluminescence analysis of glucose in real human serum samples (Mao et al., 2018).
The ability to accurately and rapidly determine blood glucose levels is crucial to the monitoring and management of diabetes in real time. In this regard, non-enzymatic glucose sensing based on nanomaterials plays an increasingly important role. An efficient, environmentally friendly, easy-to-operate and controllable two-step electrodeposition technique was employed to synthesize MOF/carbon material complexes, i.e. $\upalpha$ -CD functionalized reduced graphene oxide/Ni-based MOF ( $\overset{\cdot}{\propto}$ -CDrGO/Ni-MOF/TM) on titanium mesh (TM), where Ni-MOF acts as an electrocatalyst for glucose oxidation and rGO greatly improved the electrochemical properties of Ni-MOF. $\upalpha$ -CD successfully stemed the aggregation of reduced graphene oxide nanosheets while enhancing the composite stability, resulting in the prepared nanomaterials with sig­ nificant electrocatalytic capability for glucose. As a result, the prepared glucose sensors showed two linear dynamic ranges, $0.65\upmu\mathrm{M}-4.828\up m\mathrm{M}$ (sensitivity of $1395\upmu\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{mM}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and $4.828\mathrm{-}9.178\mathrm{mM}$ (sensitivity of $760\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{A}}\cdot\ensuremath{\mathrm{mM}}^{-1}\cdot\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2})$ with a fast response time of only $1.9\ s$ and a detection limit of $0.3\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ as well as excellent reproducibility, selectivity and stability. This constructed glucose sensing material was also satis­ fyingly utilized to determine glucose in real serum (Xu et al., 2022a).
To detect herbicides in wheat, rice and barley samples, cross-linked polyacrylonitrile/ $\gamma$ -CD-metal-organic framework $(\mathrm{PAN}/\gamma$ -CD-MOF) electrospun nanofibers were prepared by Majd et al. Satisfactory line­ arity $(\mathbf{r}^{2}\ge0.992)$ was found in the range of $2.5\mathrm{-}1250.0\mathrm{ng/mL}$ based on the best parameters (desorption solvent: acetonitrile, desorption time: 5 min, sample solution pH: 7.0, salt concentration: $15\%(w/w)$ , extraction time: $15\mathrm{min}$ ). The detection limit was $0.75{-}2.50~\mathrm{ng/mL};$ the extraction recoveries were $64.9\mathrm{-}75.7\quad\%$ , and the relative recoveries were $92.9\mathrm{-}106.1\ \%$ . The precision of the method was favorable with the relative standard deviation $\leq6.3\ \%$ . For the adsorption mechanism study, they found that the prepared materials have large porosity, great surface area, amphiphilic nanopores with nitrile and hydroxyl groups in the structure. Accordingly, the synthetic nanofiber composites could interact with the herbicides through $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, host-guest in­ teractions, H-bonds, van der Waals interactions, and hydrophobic ef­ fects. The different interactions between the nanofibers and the selected herbicides improved their extraction efficiency (Majd et al., 2022). Another study prepared a multifunctional porous carbon material through utilizing $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF as a precursor and potassium source. The comprehensive characterization results showed that the resulting car­ bon material ( $\mathrm{.}\mathrm{|}\mathrm{|}$ -CD-MOF) had a microporous structure, a large specific surface area, and plenty of potassium elements. The batch adsorption test showed that $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF had good adsorption and removal capability for four amide herbicides, possibly due to $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions. Pot experiments revealed that $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF could simultaneously adsorb herbicides and release potas­ sium ions, effectively promoting rice seedlings growth in herbicidecontaminated wastewater (Liu et al., 2019).
The detection of active ingredients in food has been one of the ob­ jectives of analysts. A sensitive electrochemical sensor based on a composite material consisting of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods, poly(3,4- ethylenedioxyethylene thiophene) (PEDOT) and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF was prepared. Under optimal conditions, the sensor exhibited a linear range of $0.4\mathrm{nM-}1800\mathrm{nM}$ for lignocaine and a detection limit of $0.1\ \mathrm{nM}$ (S/N ${}=3{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ ) due to the synergistic effect of the materials. In addition, the effects of several coexisting phenolic compounds and common metal ions on the detection of lignocaine were investigated and no significant inter­ ference was found. Finally, the sensor was favourably utilized to detect lignocaine in real chrysanthemum tea samples (Chen et al., 2022b).
The analytical detection of drugs is an extremely important study, and Luan et al. improved their conductivity by using Ketjen Black (KB) and Pt nanoparticles (PtNPs) to modify CD-MOFs. The MOF materials were found to have high sensitivity, good stability and good reproduc­ ibility in the experiments. The electrochemical sensor had a linear range of $0.08\mathrm{-}100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and a detection limit of $0.037\upmu\mathrm{M}$ $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ ) and can be successfully used for the detection ofloxacin in serum (Luan et al., 2020). For the detection of chloramphenicol, Du et al. developed novel com­ plexes of $\upbeta$ -CD functionalized graphene and Cu-1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylic acid prepared in situ. Compared with single ma­ terials, the composites not only overcome the drawbacks of graphene or
MOF itself, but also greatly improve the electrical conductivity and provide more reactive sites (Du et al., 2022).
## 3.3. Separation
With the increased emphasis on decreasing the environmental effect of chemical procedures, the advent of green chemical porous materials brings new concepts for reactant separation. The $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF material has been demonstrated to aid in the alkyl compounds isolation, including BTEX mixtures (regioisomers of benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylene), that might have an impact on the petrochemical sector. Addi­ tionally, Hartlieb et al. observed that this MOF has the capacity to segregate a broad range of combinations, such as ethylbenzene from styrene, haloaromatics, terpenes, pinenes, as well as various chiral chemicals. Compared to their unsaturated counterparts, CD-MOFs maintain saturated chemicals efficiently. Furthermore, the double bond intramolecular position affects its retention in the extended framework. According to the structural isomers of pinene and matsubei, exocyclic double-bond isomers tend to be highly likely to persist than intracyclic double-bond isomers. The halogen substituents size, as well as both analyte and framework non-covalent interactions strength, appear to be regulated by the CD-MOFs ability to differentiate varied mono- and di-substituted haloaromatic compounds, which is an obser­ vation supported by molecular simulations. The homochiral nature of CD-MOF allows it to resolve the enantiomers of chiral analytes, such as limonene and 1-phenylethanol. The above discoveries may result in chiral stationary phases for HPLC separations that are less expensive and simpler to create than existing chiral stationary phases, such as CDbonded silica particles (Hartlieb et al., 2016).
Some organochlorine pesticides are doped in the food production process. Although they play a positive role in the prevention and control of pests and diseases, they are extremely poisonous, which means they may harm the human body severely. Despite the fact that nations have severe restrictions on the presence of organochlorine pesticide residues within food, organochlorine pesticide usage has not been completely banned, and residual organochlorine pesticides can still be found. In response to this discovery, Sun et al. grafted MOFs synthesized from CD as organic ligands onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to prepare a hybrid nanocomposite. The results showed that 14 organochlorine pesticides (OCPs) (Heptachlor; Aldrin; Heptachlor epoxide B; cis-Chlordane; Endosulfan I; transChlordane; Dieldrin; ${p,p^{\prime}}$ -DDE; Endrin; Endosulfan II; ${}_{o,p}\mathrm{\prime}$ -DDT; $p,p^{\prime}$ - DDT; Methoxychlor; Mirex) with a linear range of $1{-}500~\upmu\mathrm{g/kg}$ and a correlation coefficient of 0.9991 to 1.000. The detection limit and quantification limit of the method were $0.01{-}0.04\upmu\mathrm{g/kg}$ and $0.04{-}0.12$ $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\upk g$ , respectively. The method's daily accuracy was adequate $(\mathrm{RSD\%}$ $<11.3\%)$ . OCPs in honey samples were recovered by CD-MOF/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{D}$ - SPE technique, and the recoveries varied from 76.4 to $114.3\%$ . findings showed OCPs' good selective enrichment ability within CD-MOF/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which was appropriate for honey sample D-SPE pre-treatment analysis (Sun et al., 2022a) (Fig. 5).
Environmental waters are frequently polluted by endocrine dis­ rupting compounds (EDCs), including plasticizers, surfactants, phar­ maceuticals, and personal care products. Hence, eco-friendly and sensitive methods are required to quantify the trace concentrations of these compounds. A polypropylene membrane protected- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdot}\mathbf{\upmu}$ -solid phase extraction apparatus was used to pre-concentrate EDCs (triclosan, tri­ clocarban, phenol, 4-tert-octylphenol and bisphenol A) of $\upbeta$ -CD with cross-linked citric acid. The synthesized MOF materials have higher thermal stability compared to the natural CDs. The devices equipped with $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF materials were well linear $(0.5{-}500\upmu\mathrm{g/L})$ under optimal experimental conditions. Interestingly, the recovery of EDCs was 80.2 $\%-99.9\ \%$ with low relative standard deviations $(0.1\ \%-3.8\ \%)$ and enrichment factors of 9–82 times in four different real water samples (wastewater, river water, tap water and mineral water). These results suggested that the modified device is an efficient, sensitive and ecofriendly method for the analysis of EDCs (Rozaini et al., 2022). Applying MOFs materials in capillary electrochromatographic separa­ tions, Wang et al. reported that $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF materials could provide good selectivity for 14 racemic drugs and one chiral alcohol. The capillary column coated with MOF indicated optimal detection capability for scopolamine (resolution of 17.07). Additionally, it was found that the innovative $\gamma$ -CD-MOF-coated capillary was used for quantitative test of practical samples with good reproducibility and stability (Wang et al., 2022a).
Liu and his colleagues synthesized an interesting magnetic copperbased MOF by using $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -graphene oxide and CD nanocomposite as a magnetic core and carrier for the separation of neonicotinoid in­ secticides present in aqueous environment. Experimental results demonstrated that the prepared MOF material had a large surface area $(250.33~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and great superparamagnetism with a saturation magnetization intensity of $10.47\mathrm{\emu/g}$ , and these properties can ach­ ieve rapid separation of water samples. The equilibrium data for thia­ cloprid were consistent with Langmuir monolayer adsorption model and for all other insecticides with Freundlich bilayer adsorption model (Liu et al., 2017).
## 3.4. Capture
Important industrial gases like ethylene as well as carbon dioxide are extensively employed in the petrochemical along with agricultural in­ dustries. They are often kept in high-pressure gas cylinders, which have a significant danger of explosion/leakage during storage besides trans­ portation. Particularly when just a very tiny amount is needed, such storage is exceedingly cumbersome. These limitations are efficiently avoided by the use of porous solid materials, which offer a novel method of gas storage and controlled release. Li et al. successfully synthesized two MOF materials, namely $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-Na as well as $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-K, with effective ethylene adsorption and storage stability. Both $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-Na and $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-K had ethylene encapsulation amounts of 47.4 and 52.9 $\%$ $\left({w/\mathrm{w}}\right)$ , respectively. Experiments with enhanced ripening revealed that $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF accelerated banana ripening, and this impact was com­ parable to that of pure ethylene gas. Researchers chose color and hardness changes to determine the degree of banana ripening. The experiment used $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-Na, $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-K and $1000~\upmu\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{L}$ ethylene gas to treat bananas, and it was found that all three had similar effects on banana ripening. Interestingly, it was found that $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-Na and $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-K desorbed ethylene at a slightly slower rate for the first 3 days than with ethylene gas. In the last $^4\mathrm{d}$ , the maturation difference between ethylene gas and $\upalpha$ -CD-MOFs was not significant. For hardness studies, the hardness of bananas treated with $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF-Na, $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF$\mathbb{K},$ and $1000\upmu\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{L}$ ethylene gas was essentially the same, decreasing from $8.52~\mathrm{kg/cm}^{2}$ to $1.38~\mathrm{kg/cm^{2}}$ , $2.02~\mathrm{kg/cm}^{2}$ , and $1.41~\mathrm{kg/cm^{2}}$ , respec­ tively. Overall, $\upalpha\cdot$ -CD-MOFs had similar ripening under ethylene gas ef­ ficiency. $\upalpha$ -CD-MOFs treated with ethylene gradually increased in concentration because ethylene in porous solid substrates could not be released rapidly. Under high humidity environment, the solid matrix gradually dissolved and the porous structure decomposed, allowing the encapsulated ethylene to be released gradually. The interesting findings offer a novel approach to manage fruit and vegetable ripening (Li et al., 2020a) (Fig. 6). A MOF made from $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and potassium ions was created by Wang et al. as an effective formaldehyde absorbent. Due to the interplay between its porous structure, hydrogen bonds, and host-guest interactions, the adsorption capacity and rate of $\gamma$ -CD-MOF-K were approximately 9 times more than those of activated carbon. (Wang et al., 2018). Traditional liquid alkanolamine adsorbents do not effectively absorb $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ in industrial applications, falling short of green development standards. For the first time, $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\upbeta$ -CD-MOFs were created by $\mathtt{X u}$ and colleagues by amino-functionalizing the least expensive, most easily accessible, and biocompatible $\upbeta$ -CD. It was discovered that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -β-CDMOF had an adsorption capacity of $12.3~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , which was ten times more than that of $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF. Additionally, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - $\cdot\upbeta$ -CD- $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ MOF's (947.52) selective adsorption performance was improved (Xu et al., 2019).
Fan et al. reported an efficient and simple means for preparing CDMOF monoliths (instead of powders) without using connectors or high temperatures or pressures. In contrast to $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF powder, the MOF particles in the $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF monolithic material are tightly arranged and had better moisture stability. Additionally, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption tests un­ covered that it possessed a high volume adsorption capacity for $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ , with 44.04 and $36.85\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at 0 and $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF monolithic blocks sustained their porous structure and gas adsorption capacity after $14{\mathrm{~d~}}$ exposure to a humid environment, and no essential changes had occurred in the uptake of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . This moisture-stable monolithic material has greater potential in practical use than its pow­ der counterpart in various fields involving $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption. (Fan et al., 2022).
Carbon capture and utilization or sequestration (CCUS) and direct air capture (DAC) from industrial point sources may be required to address universal climate change. It is crucial to develop next-generation sor­ bents that can reversibly capture $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ under realistic conditions in order to reduce the cost of CCUS and DAC. Carbon capture technology using amine-based sorbents faces particular problems due to their poor sta­ bility to oxygen, which is present in most emission streams of interest and at high partial pressures in the air. Sorbents containing oxy nucle­ ophilic reagents, such as hydroxide $(\mathrm{OH}^{-})$ , can react reversibly with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to form (bis)carbonate $\mathrm{(HCO}_{3}^{-}\mathrm{)}$ and should show better oxidative sta­ bility in contrast to amine-based materials. The researchers designed and prepared $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOFs in which $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ chemisorption occurs via $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ formation on the nucleophilic $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ site within the framework pores, rather than through the previously proposed pathway of carbonate or alkyl carbonate formation. In comparison to previously reported mate­ rials, among all $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOFs, the new framework $\mathrm{KHCO_{3}C D}$ -MOF exhibited rapid and high capacity of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake, excellent thermal, oxidative, and cycling stability, and selectivity in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture under complex gas conditions in dynamic breakthrough tests. Due to the low cost and excellent performance of $\mathrm{KHCO_{3}C D}$ -MOF in reality, it has enormous promising potential for CCUS (Zick et al., 2022).
Post-harvest storage of fruit has always been one of the recognized challenges. By regulating the release of different fresh-keeping gases, the decay of postharvest food can be effectively prolonged. A study demonstrated that the use of MOFs materials could capture $(56~\%$ , encapsulation efficiency) and retain hexanal for a long time $(10\mathrm{~h})$ . When each granule filled with hexanal was placed in a fruit storage box, the fresh-keeping period of mango fruits could be extended to 15 days after treatment with hexanal-containing MOFs, while the fresh-keeping period of control fruits was only 10 days (Nagarajan et al., 2021).
## 3.5. Other application
CD-MOF materials also have potential applications in other fields (Fig. 7), such as flame retardants (Zhang et al., 2021), storage mem­ ristors (Yoon et al., 2014), capacitors (Hajra et al., 2021), catalyst (Sadjadi & Koohestani, 2021), and so on.
To enhance the flame retardant properties of epoxy resins, Zhang et al. functionalized $\upbeta$ -CD-MOF materials with the help of phytate. It was reported that with only $3\%$ of the new hybrid material, the peak heat release rate (pHRR) and smoke production rate (SPR) of the composites were reduced by $41\ \%$ and $62\%$ , respectively. The release of volatiles was slowed down by increasing the loading of flame retardant to obtain swollen char as a physical barrier to close the cells. The degradation process of the epoxy resin with phosphorus as the main pyrolysis product matched with this heterocarbon was found to significantly contribute to flame suppression by FTIR spectroscopy studies. The me­ chanical properties of the composite discovered a $25\%$ increase in initial storage modulus compared to the pure epoxy resin indicating high performance fire safety without degenerating its mechanical properties. Thus, MOF-derived swelling systems create a new horizon for the application of MOFs in flame retardancy (Zhang et al., 2021).
Single crystals of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF could be injected with ionic electrolytes and flanked by silver electrodes as memristors, which could be electri­ cally converted between low and high conductivity states and persist even without applied voltage. By this means, these small pieces of nanoporous sugars acted as a non-volatile resistive random access memory storage element that could be read, erased and rewritten repeatedly. These features arised from the ionic currents inside the MOF and the deposition of a nanoscale thin passivation layer at the anode on the side of the MOF crystal. There is a critical dependence on the subnanometer width of the MOF channel, which allows only smaller ions through. In contrast, with electrolyte but without MOF, there is no memory effect (Yoon et al., 2014).
Hajra et al. selected CD as a green multifunctional material that could be incorporated into the frictional electric series and extended for its self-powered applications. The study reported the ultrasonic syn­ thesis of CD-MOF with sodium as the metal ion and cyclodextrin as the ligand for frictional electricity nanogenerators. The experiments showed three types of CD-MOFs by Kelvin probe force microscopy, exhibiting positive potentials. Depending on the surface potential, the outputs of different CD-MOF-based TENGs were $\upalpha$ -CD-MOF/Polytetrafluoro­ ethylene $>\gamma\cdot$ -CD-MOF/Polytetrafluoroethylene $>\upbeta$ -CD-MOF/Polytetra­ fluoroethylene producing electrical outputs of 152 V, $1.2~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ and 14.3 nC, respectively. The output of the fabricated devices was used to pro­ vide power supply for many low power electronics through capacitors and bridge rectifier circuits (Hajra et al., 2021). In addition to this, flexible porous carbon film supercapacitor was prepared, which was electrospun with polyacrylonitrile and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF followed by carbon­ ization at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The BET experimental results displayed that the supercapacitor retained the skeleton of the material academician and the pores formed by the spun-fibers, which were 0.73, 1.09 and 23–186 nm, respectively, showing a high specific surface area of $34.7\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . This layered porous structure ensured fast charge transfer and ion diffusion, leading to a MOF carbon electrode with a high capacity of $283.3\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ . In addition, the supercapacitor had an energy density of up to $17.5\mathrm{Wh/kg}$ and a power density of up to $6\mathrm{kW/kg}$ . Notably, it exhibited good cycling stability with a capacitance retention of $97.5~\%$ after 6000 cycles $\mathrm{(Xu,}$ Wang, et al., 2022).
Combining the characteristics of metal-organic backbone and cyclic carbohydrate polymers, Sadjadi and Koohestani prepared a novel cata­ lyst to design a green solution for organic conversion. They used CD nanosponges synthesized from $\upbeta$ -CD and hybridized with the prepared iron-based metal-organic backbone. After palladization, the composite provided a catalyst for nitroaromatic hydrogenation at ambient tem­ perature in aqueous media. The researchers hypothesized that as a nanoreactor, CD nanosponge in the catalyst backbone could encapsulate and transfer nitroaromatics in aqueous media and control Pd nano­ particle growth by acting as a capping agent. According to the experi­ ments, the catalyst's efficiency was higher than the Pd-CD nanoparticles and the MOF. The catalyst can hydrogenate steric substrates with low water solubility and substrates with competing functional groups in a cost-effective manner. Aside from that, the catalyst can be recycled several times with low Pd leaching and activity loss (Sadjadi & Koo­ hestani, 2021).
To date, most existing drugs contain chiral enantiomers. In general, enantiomers of chiral drugs exhibit different or even completely oppo­ site pharmacological, metabolic, therapeutic, or toxicological charac­ teristics in vivo. Therefore, the separation of enantiomers has always been an extremely important research topic, especially in pharmaceu­ tical analysis (Li et al., 2020b; Yang et al., 2017). Promisingly, CD-MOFs will be an edge tool to isolate and separate chiral bioactive pharma­ ceutical drugs.
## 4. Conclusion and future perspective
In terms of biosafety, traditional MOFs have great limitations in practical application due to the introduction of highly toxic metal ions during the synthesis process. In order to solve the biosafety problem of MOFs, non-toxic/degradable ligands and metal ions have been selected in the preparation process. Although the ligand molecule is CD, its safety after forming MOFs needs to be further studied and judged for wider application. Although many studies have confirmed that CD-MOFs will not cause serious toxicity to the test cells in the cell and even in animal experiments, it should be noted that when these kinds of materials are used in the human body, it still urgently needs to determine if the dosage at this time will have an impact on the examined cells. In addition, when CD-MOFs materials deliver active ingredients, whether the interaction between the two in vivo may produce undue side effects is also worth considering by subsequent researchers.
This review summarizes and discusses the processes used in the creation of CD-MOFs and the prospective uses for these materials in the delivery, separation, detection, as well as active molecules capture. In the process of analyzing the literature, it was found that the common methods for synthesizing CD-MOFs materials at this stage are the pri­ marily solvothermal method and vapor diffusion method. In the future synthesis process, the safety and simplicity of operation need to be further considered. Even if vapor diffusion is the most trustworthy, the inhalation of organic vapor still needs to be avoided when using this method. The drug-carrying capacity of CD-MOFs materials is better than that of general carriers, it will be useful for the effective adsorption and removal of pesticides as well as the separation and purification of iso­ mers. The use of CD-MOFs for organic gas collection and medication delivery is currently in the laboratory research phase, and larger-scale experimental studies are required to obtain practical application value.
On the basis of summarizing the past studies, it is easy to observe that CD-MOFs can encapsulate active substances more rapidly and efficiently compared with the encapsulation of a single CD. At the same time, CDMOFs play a role in improving the adverse physicochemical properties, solubility, release rate, and bioavailability of active substances. How­ ever, the CD-MOFs application concerned with food along with phar­ maceutical fields is still in its initial stage, and the following suggestions are made for future research directions: 1) Utilizing metal ions that are biocompatible, CD-MOFs may be created $({\mathrm{Fe}}^{3+},{\mathrm{~\bar{M}g^{2+},~{\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}}}.$ , etc.) in high valence state, so as to achieve controlled preparation of crystal structure and size; 2) in terms of encapsulation and delivery of active substances, pragmatic guidelines should be introduced and enforced for exploring a reasonable matching scheme for the encapsulation and de­ livery of the active substance, so as to facilitate the rapid selection of suitable CD-MOFs, encapsulation methods and encapsulation conditions with the knowledge of the structure and properties of the active sub­ stance; 3) developing non-toxic, simple and effective techniques to in­ crease CD-MOF stability in various media; 4) based on CD-MOF physicochemical research, examining the stability, sensory, and quali­ tative characteristics of CD-MOFs; 5) attempting to promote larger-scale application of CD-MOFs, such as food, pharmaceutical, antiseptic and detection fields.
In the future preparation application, it will be fascinating to analyze different environmental variables first, which means that intelligent CDMOFs materials will be prepared according to various stimuli in the environment. Meanwhile, this strategy can also reduce the use of preparation materials to achieve the purpose of protecting the envi­ ronment. In addition, when CD-MOFs are used in the pharmaceutical and food industries, these materials are inevitably in contact with food or organisms. Therefore, careful consideration should always be given to the interaction between this kind of carrier and the human body, food, and to whether the carrier will affect the normal activities of organisms after entering the organism. Most of the multi-stimuli-responsive sys­ tems reported so far are proof of concept studies, in which the carriers are usually non-biodegradable and not suitable for in vivo applications. Consequently, intelligent multi-stimuli-responsive CD-MOFs research is still in its infancy, and the number of documents involved is also limited so it is impossible to summarize and prospect this kind of research at this stage. Nevertheless, we still need to accumulate more research to solve some key problems, and then the application of CD-MOFs will expand prospectively.
Although CD-MOFs have many advantages, their instability in water severely limits their application. As the MOF backbone collapses and dissolves in water in an aqueous environment, thus losing its original function. Some studies have indicated that the water solubility of CDMOF can be reduced in different ways, and these materials have only appeared in the experimental stage. For the purpose of fundamentally enhance the CD-MOF stability, it is necessary to prepare functionalized CD derivatives from the original material to promote the stability of the MOF backbone. Alternatively, hydrophobic treatment can be applied to the surface of the MOF material, but this may destroy pore size of the MOF material.
In terms of CD-MOFs materials preparation, there is a need to resume simpler and easier skills to regulate the size (macro to nano) of the crystals of CD-MOFs materials. At this stage, crystal structure including the size and shape has a significant influence on the toxicity of materials used for drug delivery. In addition, the surfaces and porosities of these materials are critical characteristics, nevertheless they may not be able to resist harsh environments. New approaches to increase their struc­ tural stability, such as functionalizing the chemical environment of the active part by modification, forming core/shell structures and cleaning superfluous solvents from the pores after preparation, will be bound to promote CD-MOFs' application more effectively or even boost their performance to a new level.
Recently, machine learning has gradually emerged as a new and important means to explain correlations and patterns that cannot be easily identified by relying on traditional techniques. Although many machine learning papers on CD-MOF materials have been published at this stage, the application of this approach is still in its nascent stage. To better improve and expand the application of machine learning in developing and predicting the properties of CD-MOFs materials, a reli­ able database from published papers is needed urgently. Additionally, computational chemistry has been applied to perform machine learning, and prediction by computational methods is relatively easy and avail­ able with large amounts of data. The use of this approach facilitates machine learning as a new and favorable direction in the study of CDMOFs. Recently, the construction of neural networks for describing the potential energy surfaces of various chemical entities has been successfully demonstrated. These “neural network potentials” are impressive and encouraging, because their accuracy is comparable to that of DFT methods, but they are significantly faster than DFT calcu­ lations. However, the accuracy is slightly lower using traditional mo­ lecular simulation methods. Therefore, the use of neural network-based methods is bound to greatly improve the accuracy of computational methods and better facilitate the prediction of CD-MOFs performance. The current stage of research work is basically at the point where re­ searchers perform machine learning in a supervised state. In the future, researchers expect that other learning modalities such as semisupervised learning or unsupervised learning will be more actively incorporated into CD-MOFs research. In semi-supervised learning, the machine learning model itself predicts and determines the labels of a large amount of unlabeled data by learning from a small fraction of the labeled data. This semi-supervised learning is very beneficial when considering a large number of MOFs, especially for properties that require direct experiments or expensive DFT-level calculations (e.g., catalytic activity, electrical conductivity). Using unsupervised learning, where no labels are given in the provided data, machine learning models can discover previously unknown trends in CD-MOFs. This will contribute to a deeper understanding of the intrinsic properties of these materials. It is foreseeable that generative models will play a more direct and important role in the design of new CD-MOFs.
